      "Language": "spa",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The last modifications of the National Cholesterol Education Program (NCEP) indicate some lower therapeutic targets of low density protein cholesterol (LDL-c) in patients with very high vascular risk. Our study aims to establish the degree of compliance of the therapeutic targets defined by these modifications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHOD",
          "content": "Descriptive and cross sectional study of patients from a cardiovascular clinic. Low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-c) was calculated and targets were established according the new update of NCEP-ATP III."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "836 patients, 625 (75%) with HR and 211(25%) with VHR were studied 45% of HR and 67% of VHR were receiving treatment with statins (p < 0.001) but only 38% of HR and 15% of VHR achieved target LDL-c levels. The percentage of patients who could achieve LDL-goals using the maximum approved dose of the statins plus other lipid lowering drug was calculated: The 31% of HR and 51% of VHR were unable to achieve the LDL-c targets."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Only 15% of very high risk patients achieve target levels of LDL-c according to the new NCEP-ATP III recommendations. Even using maximum dose of statins in combination with other lipid lowering drugs, less than half of the patients will be able to achieve LDL-c goal (< 70 mg/dl)."
        }
      ]},
